    <body>
      <sec id="article-29777.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Suture hypersensitivity is an uncommon but clinically significant immunologic reaction in which suture material acts as a foreign antigen, triggering an exaggerated immune response. This reaction may result in inflammation, erythema, delayed wound healing, and complications that can mimic postoperative infection. Individuals with a history of allergies, autoimmune disorders, or genetic predispositions such as certain human leukocyte antigen profiles may have an increased risk. Because hypersensitivity reactions can occur in both superficial and deep tissues, clinicians must be able to distinguish them from normal wound healing processes and infectious complications to ensure appropriate management and avoid unnecessary treatments.</p>
        <p>This continuing education activity reviews the pathophysiology, clinical presentation, evaluation, and management of suture hypersensitivity. Participants learn to recognize risk factors, identify signs and symptoms, and evaluate patients using clinical assessment and, when appropriate, diagnostic tools such as patch testing or imaging for deeper tissue involvement. The activity also highlights management strategies, including removal or replacement of the offending suture material and the use of anti-inflammatory therapies. Emphasis is placed on interprofessional collaboration among surgeons, dermatologists, allergists/immunologists, and other healthcare professionals to promote early diagnosis, reduce complications, and optimize wound healing through a coordinated, patient-centered approach.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Screen patients for predisposing factors like allergies or autoimmune conditions that increase the risk of hypersensitivity.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement appropriate wound care protocols to manage hypersensitivity reactions and promote healing.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess the impact of suture material on patient outcomes and adjust treatment plans accordingly.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate and communicate with the interprofessional team to manage hypersensitivity reactions and improve outcomes for&#x000a0;affected patients.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=29777&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=29777">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Hypersensitivity is an immune-mediated reaction&#x000a0;involving a disproportionate response&#x000a0;from the&#x000a0;immune system&#x000a0;to an antigen, causing inflammation and tissue damage. Specific human leukocyte antigen genes increase susceptibility to a hypersensitivity reaction. Hypersensitivity reactions are divided into 4 subtypes based on the&#x000a0;immune mechanism. Suture hypersensitivity is a type IV delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction, mediated by a T-cell&#x000a0;activation&#x000a0;24 to 72 hours post-placement, resulting in localized inflammation and dermatitis. Deep tissues may be affected by a hypersensitivity reaction in the&#x000a0;absence of superficial findings, leading to delayed detection.&#x000a0;A suture hypersensitivity reaction&#x000a0;can cause wound dehiscence and&#x000a0;is similar in appearance to a wound infection,&#x000a0;leading to unnecessary antibiotic use.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>Suture hypersensitivity is most common with synthetic suture or nickel-containing stainless steel. In addition to the suture material itself, materials used in processing the suture,&#x000a0;including dyes, may also cause a hypersensitivity reaction. Symptoms include erythema and edema, often accompanied by papules or nodules.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Treatment of a suture hypersensitivity reaction usually involves removal of the foreign body. Severe reactions may benefit from topical or systemic corticosteroids.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Consultation with allergists&#x000a0;or dermatologists may be appropriate&#x000a0;to assist with diagnosis and management. Prompt recognition is key, and follow-up may include patch testing.</p>
        <p>Specific perioperative screening panels are not well established, but those with a history of hypersensitivity can be referred for evaluation and recommendations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Complications from suture hypersensitivity reactions increase hospitalization days and healthcare costs. Collaboration with the interprofessional team,&#x000a0;including dermatologists, allergists, immunologists, and enterostomal therapy nurses, is necessary for the early recognition, diagnosis, and treatment&#x000a0;of suture hypersensitivity reactions.&#x000a0;Please see our module on&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/30717">Type IV Hypersensitivity</ext-link>.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The tissue response to suture depends on the material (absorbable versus non-absorbable, synthetic versus biologic), tissue type, and the immune system. Materials commonly implicated&#x000a0;in a hypersensitivity response include chromic gut, nylon or vicryl, and, less commonly, polypropylene (Prolene). An immune response may be provoked to antigens within the suture or to residual particulates from suture processing. The character of the suture material, including the number of filaments and the pattern of decay, affects the response.</p>
        <p>While an <bold>innate immune response</bold> may drive the initial reaction, the <bold>acquired response</bold> mediated by T cells predominates. In a type IV hypersensitivity response, T cells respond to an antigen by secreting cytokines and activating macrophages and cytotoxic T cells.&#x000a0;In some instances, type one hypersensitivity, which is immunoglobulin E-mediated, may play a role in those with atopy&#x000a0;who have been sensitized to a product.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Suture Types </bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Absorbable sutures</bold>, such as gut or polyglactin (absorbable, braided synthetic, often marketed as Vicryl), exploit the immune response to achieve suture dissolution, stimulating&#x000a0;macrophage-activating T cells and an enzymatic cascade that degrades the material over days to weeks. In an effort to wall off a foreign body, activated macrophages differentiate into epithelioid cells and aggregate to form multinucleated giant cells,&#x000a0; producing granulomatous inflammation. Silk, catgut, and chromicized catgut&#x000a0;are often the most reactive sutures. Such reactions are less common with synthetic absorbable sutures such as polyglactin 910 and poliglecaprone 25.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Suture diameter and the duration of the absorption interval contribute to the risk of developing a hypersensitivity reaction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;In absorbable sutures, chromic salts and collagen often stimulate an immune response, and coating agents such as antibiotics, antiseptics, or dyes can cause a hypersensitivity reaction.&#x000a0;Likewise, non-absorbable suture is also treated, dyed, and coated with various products.</p>
        <p>Nylon, polypropylene, polyester, and silk sutures predominate.&#x000a0;Ethylene oxide, used to sterilize some non-absorbable sutures, is&#x000a0;known to cause a hypersensitivity reaction.&#x000a0;The risk of allergic reaction is low in the case of non-absorbable stitches, such as polyamide 6/6 and nylon, both containing the monomer e-caprolactam. Hypersensitivity reactions have been reported in cases where polypropylene and sterilized sutures with ethylene oxide and triclosan were used. The addition of dye for coloring sutures has also been reported as a cause of hypersensitivity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Silk sutures</bold> can&#x000a0;stimulate 2 different types of reactions. In the most common type, the suture is surrounded by a fibrous tissue capsule of variable thickness, accompanied by a layer of histiocytes from&#x000a0;1 to&#x000a0;3 cell layers deep. Giant cells and lymphocytes are variably present, and capillaries are more prominent with a greater cellular response.</p>
        <p>In the acquired response, the capsule is present, but fibroblasts and histiocytes invade the interstices of the suture. In rare instances, the response resembles a granuloma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Monofilament nylon suture occasionally causes a hypersensitivity reaction and becomes&#x000a0;surrounded by compact, fibrous tissue. Histiocytes&#x000a0;cluster adjacent to the suture occasionally, and giant cells rarely. No fragmentation of the nylon suture is seen.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Acute and Chronic Phases of Reactions</bold>
</p>
        <p>A suture hypersensitivity reaction&#x000a0;can be divided into acute and chronic phases.&#x000a0;The <bold>chronic phase</bold> is specific to the type of suture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Giant cell formation, an inflammatory reaction to foreign material, is thought to underlie the various clinical manifestations of suture hypersensitivity. Acute suture hypersensitivity is seen within 48 hours after placement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Following the <bold>acute phase</bold>, absorption begins for the absorbable suture. Reaction to synthetic polymers,&#x000a0;which are dissolved via hydrolysis, is less pronounced than to intestine-derived suture. The exception is a synthetic&#x000a0;absorbable suture placed within the conjunctiva or oral cavity, which can&#x000a0;promote edema and discomfort.&#x000a0;Reaction to gut suture is characterized by a thin capsule of connective tissue&#x000a0;with a few histiocytes and lymphocytes forming a fibrous capsule around the suture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>After absorption, the site is marked by a collection of monocytes with characteristic brown, foamy cytoplasm.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Non-absorbable sutures are encapsulated by a rim of connective tissue with surrounding histiocytes, giant cells, and lymphocytes, most commonly seen with silk and cotton sutures, less often with dacron, and least with nylon and wire.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;By the third day of tissue contact with non-absorbable sutures, few fibroblasts are present, and the intensity of leukocyte infiltration decreases.&#x000a0;The presence of monocytes, giant cells, round cells, and polymorphonuclear cells gradually decreases. Fibroblasts penetrate between the silk fibers and eventually form a fibrous capsule around the suture material.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hypersensitivities</bold>
</p>
        <p>The extent of the hypersensitivity reaction also depends on the tissue receiving the suture. For example, silk and non-absorbable braided or monofilament polyester sutures precipitate bladder stones when&#x000a0;placed within bladder mucosa,&#x000a0;whereas monofilament polypropylene and plain catgut do not.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;The development of a foreign body granuloma may be associated with an immunoreaction of macrophages.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;Distinguishing true allergy from granuloma is important. Suture granuloma is common, and suture selection may reduce the risk of formation. If a firm nodule develops weeks or even months after suture placement, a granuloma is likely. Diagnosis is confirmed by clinical assessment and, if indicated, microbiological testing.</p>
        <p>Tissue adhesive can also cause a hypersensitivity reaction. A 2024 systematic review compiled 193 reported cases of&#x000a0;allergic contact dermatitis&#x000a0;following wound closure with 2-octyl cyanoacrylate tissue adhesive.&#x000a0;These adhesives have additional constituents that can cause a reaction, including dyes, plasticizers, formaldehyde, and other ingredients.&#x000a0;When applied on the epidermis with a broken skin barrier, the cyanoacrylate may be exposed to antigen-presenting cells, thus causing a reaction.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Cross-reactivity has also been demonstrated with methyl and ethyl cyanoacrylate, also used in some medical&#x000a0;and commercial applications. A hypersensitivity reaction to surgical staples is usually due to the nickel component. Staples comprised of surgical-grade steel with little or no nickel result in less frequent hypersensitivity reactions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In the setting of a reaction following concurrent use of suture and tissue adhesive, a linear, pruritic rash&#x000a0;that follows the outline of the adhesive&#x000a0;is likely secondary to the adhesive rather than the suture. Additional materials related to hypersensitivity reactions are acrylic and resin, commonly found on adhesives, plastics, paints, and inks, and&#x000a0;allergens commonly found in adhesives are colophony, abietol, abietic acid, benzoyl peroxide, rubber accelerators, and antioxidants. Causes of prior sensitization with acrylates include manicure&#x000a0;products and bone and dental cement. Hypersensitivity reactions while using&#x000a0;a hydrocolloid dressing are due to colophonium.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r14">[14]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Suture hypersensitivity reactions occur&#x000a0;in approximately 1% of cases following suture placement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;The reported rate of nickel sensitization in Western populations is&#x000a0;as high as 20%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Immune status&#x000a0;and genetic makeup impact the immunologic response to suture antigens.&#x000a0;Those with autoimmune disorders or atopy are more likely to develop hypersensitivity reactions.</p>
        <p>Conversely, immunosuppressed individuals may be less likely to develop hypersensitivity. In one study, suture specimens were obtained from 12 renal transplant patients who had undergone renal transplant at various times,&#x000a0;ranging from months to years in the past. All were taking azathioprine, and the majority were taking corticosteroids. Samples from catgut, silk, nylon, and synthetic polyester were examined. Response to the suture was limited to a small fibroblastic or histiocytic response. In some tissue sections, the stitches appeared to have displaced adjacent healthy tissue without any cellular response.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0;Direct cytotoxic effects of suture are even more rare,&#x000a0;and limited to case reports.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Cutaneous hypersensitivity involves acute or delayed-type reactions.&#x000a0;An immediate hypersensitivity reaction is immunoglobulin E (IgE)-mediated and results in histamine release,&#x000a0;causing hives and itching.&#x000a0;A delayed-type response occurring approximately 24 to 72 hours after exposure is mediated by T cells and activated macrophages that gather at the site and&#x000a0;secrete cytokines. The mechanism of IgE-mediated reactions is well-studied, but the process by which delayed-type reactions occur in the skin is less well understood. Suture hypersensitivity is considered a delayed-type reaction.</p>
        <p>Contact dermatitis is the most commonly referenced form of delayed-type hypersensitivity reaction. Characterized by an acute phase during which erythema, vesiculation, and pruritus occur, contact dermatitis is initiated when the skin&#x000a0;comes into contact with the allergen.&#x000a0;In the case of a hypersensitivity to suture, a hapten from the suture material binds to a larger skin protein to generate an immune response.&#x000a0;When&#x000a0;the hapten&#x000a0;contacts the skin, it is taken up via pinocytosis or endocytosis by the antigen-presenting cells in the skin.</p>
        <p>The hapten and carrier protein form a new antigen that is presented to T cells by the major histocompatibility complex, and a costimulatory molecule that upregulates the expression of surface molecules and cytokines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;The primary antigen-presenting cells are the Langerhans cells in the dermis and epidermis, which, following activation, migrate to the lymph nodes, presenting the antigen-major histocompatibility complex (MHC) to a T-cell, which then recruits macrophages and secretes cytokines.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>T-cell activation requires 2 signals. The first signal is the binding of the antigen&#x02013;MHC complex presented on an antigen-presenting cell to the T-cell receptor.&#x000a0;The second signal is triggered when the costimulatory molecule on the antigen-presenting cell interacts with its corresponding T-cell receptor. Upon activation,&#x000a0;T cells differentiate into CD4+ or CD8+ cells. The&#x000a0;T-cell transforms into a memory T-cell,&#x000a0;allowing it to respond rapidly to subsequent exposure. Upon re-exposure to the antigen, CD4+ cells secrete inflammatory cytokines, and CD8+ cells exert a direct cytotoxic effect.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Keratinocytes are key components of the skin's innate immune function, stimulating an inflammatory cascade in response to cytokine secretion, recognizing antigens via Toll-like receptors, and interacting with Langerhans cells and T cells.&#x000a0;Keratinocytes both receive signaling from and send signaling to T cells.&#x000a0;Upon activation, keratinocytes present antigens to T cells, thereby serving both as a barrier and as an active immune&#x000a0;moderator.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Keratinocytes also release proinflammatory cytokines, including interleukins, interferons, and tumor necrosis factor. In conjunction with nitric oxide, these cytokines recruit lymphocytes, macrophages, and neutrophils to the site, producing an inflammatory cascade and a direct cytotoxic effect. Barrier disruption&#x000a0;triggers cytokine release from keratinocytes,&#x000a0;amplifying inflammation and recruiting additional T cells. Within injured tissue, lymphocytes upregulate keratinocytes,&#x000a0;which recruit lymphocytes in a positive feedback loop.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Lymphocyte cytokine production stimulates the release of keratinocyte&#x000a0;chemokines, which attract additional lymphocytes, such as neutrophils, to the site of Injury or inflammation. Specific cytokines released by keratinocytes also selectively recruit eosinophils, basophils, and mast cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Tissue damage promotes the release of reactive oxygen species, which, in sufficient quantity, cause increased tissue destruction and necrosis. Relatively low levels of reactive oxygen species induce apoptosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r20">[20]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>A preoperative history should include any known allergies or prior reactions to suture materials, adhesives, or metals. Risk factors for a hypersensitivity reaction include a history of atopic dermatitis, prior adhesive exposure, body mass index of 30 or greater, type II diabetes, and prior surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;When assessing active skin reactions, it is important to consider the timing of onset, symptoms, and potential exposures.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Physical exam may reveal&#x000a0;a localized erythematous, pruritic&#x000a0;rash and draining papulovesicular eruptions in proximity to the surgical site. Delayed presentation may reveal extrusion of the suture through an incision.&#x000a0;Hypersensitivity reactions can lead to complications, including adhesions and obstruction, wound dehiscence,&#x000a0;secondary infection, and granuloma formation.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A hypersensitivity reaction may present in a delayed fashion and will not respond to antibiotics.&#x000a0;A granulomatous reaction may form months after placement of the suture and will present as firm nodules.&#x000a0;When&#x000a0;evaluating a postoperative wound disruption or eruption, consider a suture reaction as a possible cause.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Diagnosis of a suture hypersensitivity reaction usually relies on direct examination, and the onset correlates with the placement of suture material. Evaluation includes a tabulation of all exposures; a distinction between suture hypersensitivity, infection, and&#x000a0;expected wound healing is important.&#x000a0;Immediate additional testing is usually not required, but subsequent patch testing may help clarify the source of reactivity. Allergen testing may also be conducted by re-exposing the skin to an antigen via a single interrupted suture into the dermis.&#x000a0;In cases involving&#x000a0;a known history of malignancy, it may be necessary to rule out recurrence or metastatic disease.</p>
        <p>Patch testing, such as the American Contact Dermatitis Society Core allergen series, the North American Contact Dermatitis Group patch test, and the thin-layer, rapid-use epicutaneous test, is an important tool&#x000a0;to identify a&#x000a0;specific allergen. No commercial patch test screens for 2-octyl cyanoacrylate. Testing for cyanoacrylate sensitivity&#x000a0;utilizes&#x000a0;cyanoacrylate diluted in petrolatum, undiluted cyanoacrylate, or the commercial product itself, such as Dermabond.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>When the specific adhesive component causing sensitivity is unclear, patch testing should include&#x000a0;methacrylates&#x000a0;and other ingredients such as formaldehyde, benzalkonium&#x000a0;chloride,&#x000a0;and cyanoacrylate polymerization inhibitors.&#x000a0;Using a higher concentration of adhesive and pre-abrading the skin yields more accurate results.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Ethylene oxide, used to sterilize sutures and other materials, can cause a hypersensitivity reaction and therefore should be included in patch testing.&#x000a0;Epichlorohydrin has high cross-reactivity to ethylene oxide and can be used for this purpose.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r4">[4]</xref></p>
        <p>When specific patch testing agents are unavailable in isolation, it is recommended to allergen test with the suture placed through the skin.&#x000a0;Sonographic evaluation is useful for suspected cases involving deeper tissues. Suture granulomas present as irregular, nodular hypoechoic lesions with a double hyper-echoic line, representing&#x000a0;the suture material, within the dermis or Subcutaneous tissues.&#x000a0;Active granulomas&#x000a0;may exhibit&#x000a0;slight or moderate peripheral vascularization.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>The primary goals of treatment include removing the inflammatory stimulus, treating the associated skin effects, and&#x000a0;associated symptoms such as pruritus and drainage.&#x000a0;Severe cases may require extended systemic therapy.&#x000a0;Rarely will a secondary infection occur that will require topical or systemic antibiotics.</p>
        <p>Treatment&#x000a0;of suture hypersensitivity depends on the severity of the reaction and its&#x000a0;impact on&#x000a0;wound healing, any secondary complications, and the time interval from initial placement. Often, removal of the allergen is required, plus or minus local or systemic treatment for any skin eruption. In moderate to severe reactions, a topical or systemic corticosteroid and antihistamine are indicated.</p>
        <p>Treatment for hypersensitivity to surgical glue usually requires dissolution of the glue using acetone and application of a topical corticosteroid as needed. In a&#x000a0;severe&#x000a0;reaction,&#x000a0;systemic steroids/antihistamines may be indicated, and a small subset requires washout and wide debridement. Additional therapies include topical immune response modifiers and moisturizers. Recording the reaction in the medical record so these products and any products that might cross-react can be avoided in the future is important.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Suture hypersensitivity can mimic primary granulomatous disorders, surgical site infection, abscess, wound dehiscence, cellulitis, or drug eruption, and may exist concurrently with a&#x000a0;wound infection or dehiscence. Symptoms often appear days to weeks postoperatively and may be mistaken for any of the above. Any clinical suspicion for malignancy or infection can be assessed with a biopsy or wound culture.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r8">[8]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Overall&#x000a0;prognosis depends on the tissue involved and the severity of the&#x000a0;reaction, but&#x000a0;in most circumstances,&#x000a0;suture hypersensitivity is a self-limiting condition without significant consequences other than increasing sensitivity to the allergy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>The complications of a suture hypersensitivity reaction depend on the type of suture material used and the tissue being sutured. The extent of inflammation, edema, and foreign body reaction may complicate wound healing in any tissue and cause postoperative adhesions, wound dehiscence, infection, and&#x000a0;bowel obstruction.&#x000a0;With the introduction of a foreign body into tissues, there is a possibility of bacterial colonization&#x000a0;that may lead to active infections&#x000a0;with agents such as&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Staphylococcus epidermidis</italic>. Suture may provoke a lithogenic nidus in the urinary system, which can lead to obstruction and infection. Suture hypersensitivity may cause the breakdown of vascular and gastrointestinal anastomosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s12" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>In the event of a complicated reaction or an unknown allergen or allergens, consultation with an allergist or dermatologist may be helpful.&#x000a0;A wound specialist may be needed to help care for complex wounds.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>If the patient has a history of known allergies is important to avoid similar&#x000a0;allergens and prevent further complications. Informed consent should include the signs and symptoms of suture hypersensitivity, such as erythema, edema, pruritis, nodular mass, vesicular eruption, extrusion of the suture through an incision, and seroma. The patient is advised to&#x000a0;contact the&#x000a0;care team to discuss any concerns or complications. Patient compliance with wound care is important&#x000a0;for proper wound healing.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r26">[26]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s14" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>The&#x000a0;frequent use of products for processing and treating surgical closure equipment can lead to increased incidents of hypersensitivity reactions to these materials. Obtaining a complete and accurate history of any such prior reaction can help guide the selection of skin closure materials or refer for additional testing before application of any of these materials. Preoperative patch testing as needed may help avoid postoperative hypersensitivity reactions, wound dehiscence, and additional complications.&#x000a0;For example, anyone with a known history of metal allergy should not be exposed to surgical staples.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The likelihood of a suture hypersensitivity reaction can be mitigated by suture selection and careful patient history.&#x000a0;Silk sutures are often selected due to their relatively low cost, lower reactivity, ease of sterilization, and greater strength compared to other sutures.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;Asthma, interstitial cystitis, and necrotizing granulomas are&#x000a0;correlated with delayed reactions to silk, treated&#x000a0;by removal.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Vicryl sutures&#x000a0;have low antigenicity,&#x000a0;generate sparse inflammatory infiltrates,&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;hypersensitivity is very rare.</p>
        <p>When comparing silk fibroin adhesive with cyanoacrylate mesh adhesive in total joint arthroplasty, there was a statistically significant increase in allergic contact dermatitis associated with the use of&#x000a0;cyanoacrylate&#x000a0;adhesive. In contrast, silk fibroin adhesive&#x000a0;was better tolerated while providing effective wound closure. Based on these findings, silk fibroin adhesive appears to be a superior wound closure option to consider for patients undergoing total joint arthroplasty, particularly for those at higher risk of skin sensitivity or adhesive-related complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r28">[28]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Early identification and management of suture hypersensitivity reactions can minimize complications and promote timely healing. Swift recognition and treatment for a suture hypersensitivity reaction requires a collaborative approach among the interprofessional team to optimize patient-centered care and improve outcomes. Surgeons, primary care clinicians, dermatologists, nurses, and other health professionals involved in the care should possess the clinical skills and knowledge to diagnose and manage a suture hypersensitivity reaction, including the ability to promptly diagnose and treat any such reaction. An experienced interprofessional team can improve early detection and treatment of any suture hypersensitivity reaction.</p>
        <p>A strategic approach to help minimize a suture hypersensitivity reaction in those with a history of allergic reactions is important, including early consultation with an allergist or dermatologist to strategize for the best outcome, including avoiding&#x000a0;products containing potential allergens. Patch testing can be conducted preoperatively or if an unexpected reaction occurs, in the postoperative setting.&#x000a0;Once the allergen is identified and removed, treatment&#x000a0;is initiated, and, if indicated,&#x000a0;the team arranges for outpatient wound care.</p>
        <p>Excellent interprofessional communication is critical, allowing for collaborative decision-making.&#x000a0;Coordination of care ensures timely diagnosis, treatment, and follow-up, minimizes errors, and enhances patient safety. Interprofessional team collaboration in decision-making and communication is a key element for a good outcome. Patients with suture hypersensitivity have better prognoses and outcomes when signs and symptoms are identified early.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-29777.r29">[29]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-29777.s16">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
